  "markdown_report": "The best first experiment is a distance-resolved diagnostic adapter for the supplied NextItNet. At 0% progress, establishing trustworthy measurements is more useful than changing the backbone without evidence.\n\n**Insights from the starting point**\n\n1. Existing margins measure sampled training separation. They cannot explain the reported preference for specific recent matches over broadly relevant recommendations.\n2. Left padding makes readout correctness essential. Selecting `count−1` can inspect padding or an earlier interaction and invalidate subsequent diagnosis.\n3. Available text supports lexical evidence, while histories support behavioral sensitivity. Neither establishes compatibility, satisfied needs, latent intent, or simulator traits.\n\n**Insights from related work**\n\n1. NextItNet’s causal dilations impose a calculable receptive field [1]. Distance-specific perturbations can check whether observed sensitivity agrees with that architecture.\n2. Shuffling studies question assumptions about sequential structure [2]. Controlled perturbations should therefore measure dependence without interpreting every change as preference drift.\n3. Sampled recommendation metrics can reverse model comparisons [3]. The pinned macro validation HR@10 must remain the selection criterion; diagnostic margins cannot replace it.\n\n**Framework and directions**\n\n| Direction | Evidence | Gap addressed |\n|---|---|---|\n| Implementation integrity | Padding, future-token invariance | Incorrect readout or noncausal operations |\n| Temporal dependence | Recent removal, older removal, prefix shuffle | Unknown reliance on recent versus older events |\n| Evidence quality | Supports, missing text, unavailable values | Unsupported behavioral interpretations |\n\n**Candidate ideas and assessments**\n\nScores are originality/potential/code difficulty; higher difficulty means more implementation work.\n\n- **Distance-resolved adapter — 5/8/3.** Originality: architecture-linked controls are useful, although perturbation probes are established. Potential: creates actionable hypotheses, although sensitivity is not causal evidence. Difficulty: reuses existing hooks, although padding and paired scoring require care [1,2].\n- **Recent-suffix auxiliary loss — 4/6/4.** Originality: applies familiar augmentation to the diagnosis, although it is incremental. Potential: may strengthen recent evidence, although it can suppress persistent preferences. Difficulty: shares one backbone and head, although another forward pass costs training budget [1,2].\n- **Support-aware lexical audit — 3/5/3.** Originality: connects title evidence with ranking, although lexical overlap is conventional. Potential: exposes vague matches, although it cannot verify practical fit. Difficulty: needs no pretrained weights, although identifier extraction needs safeguards [4].\n\n**Selected algorithm**\n\nFreeze each category’s actual model and compare paired positive-minus-negative margins under original histories, final-item-preserving shuffles, and equal-size recent/older removals. Reapply left padding and score the same original target at the final nonpadding position. Report paired margin changes, sampled-pair reversals, support, and perturbation distance; do not call these HR@10 or confidence estimates.\n\nRetain existing all-position probes separately. Check causality by altering positions after an interior position and comparing its features. Mark unsupported measurements unavailable, preserve model mode, and leave unavailable category breakdowns empty. Interpret temporal failures as hypotheses for subsequent single-model ablations, never as established intent.\n\nReferences: [1] NextItNet; [2] Sequential-structure analysis (2024); [3] Krichene and Rendle; [4] ECCR (2024), linked below.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Implement a distance-resolved, support-aware DiagnosisProbe around the actual trained NextItNet, using controlled history perturbations and paired scores to distinguish implementation failures from temporal-dependence hypotheses.",
    "motivation": "The supplied diagnosis reports broad relevance and excessive persistence of older interests, but current training-margin probes do not measure those behaviors. An inexpensive adapter can establish whether the model uses reachable recent and older events before spending the shared GPU budget on architectural changes.",
    "implementation_notes": "Preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Use view.model for mode management and view.log2feats, view.item_emb, and view.predict_score without replacing the host ModelView. Keep existing all-position margins separate from new final-position probes. Select each row's largest index with sequence != 0; never use count−1. Require a nonzero positive and negative at the selected position, and exclude perturbations that would empty the history. Recent and older deletions must remove equal numbers of valid events while retaining the final item, with support reported for every comparison. Restore left padding after deletion. Bootstrap paired deltas at user level only if stable user identifiers are available; otherwise omit intervals rather than assume batch rows are independent users. Compute receptive field from the actual convolution stack and qualify its interpretation if cross-position operations exist. Preserve and restore model mode, use no_grad, and use a local diagnostic RNG. Empty samples should return null with support zero. Use only permitted titles/descriptions for optional lexical coverage; do not access unavailable ratings, reviews, or item categories. This first experiment leaves the supplied training architecture and predictor writer unchanged and retains one checkpoint per category. Any later training change must update both architecture definitions consistently and retain all prescribed seeds, budgets, routing, splits, and evaluator settings.",
    "pseudocode": "class DiagnosisProbe:\n    def run_full_diagnosis(train_loader):\n        previous_mode = view.model.training\n        view.model.eval()\n        try:\n            with no_grad():\n                collect_existing_embedding_and_all_position_margin_metrics()\n                for _, sequence, positive, negative in bounded_batches(train_loader):\n                    t = largest_nonzero_index_per_row(sequence)\n                    valid = valid_final_targets(t, positive, negative)\n                    targets = paired_items_at_original_t(positive, negative, t)\n                    base = paired_margin_at_last_nonpadding(sequence, targets)\n                    for control in [shuffle_prefix_keep_last,\n                                    remove_recent_nonfinal,\n                                    remove_equal_size_oldest]:\n                        changed, support = control(sequence, local_rng_seed_42)\n                        changed = left_pad_valid_events(changed)\n                        margin = paired_margin_at_last_nonpadding(changed, targets)\n                        record_paired_deltas_and_reversals(base, margin, valid & support)\n                    check_future_changes_leave_interior_features_unchanged(sequence)\n                return {'metrics': summarize_with_support_and_nulls(),\n                        'metric_definitions': explicit_definitions_and_limits()}\n        finally:\n            view.model.train(previous_mode)\n\ndef diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis):\n    return prompt_with_metrics_definitions_and_instruction_to_separate_measurements_from_hypotheses(raw_diagnosis)",
    "originality": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "Combines NextItNet receptive-field checks, left-padding integrity, and matched perturbations into a task-specific diagnostic protocol.",
      "negative": "Its constituent techniques are established; novelty lies mainly in their disciplined integration."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 8,
      "positive": "Provides a reusable basis for selecting controlled single-model ablations and detecting invalid diagnostic interpretations.",
      "negative": "Training-sample sensitivity may not predict validation improvements or explain real purchase requirements."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 3,
      "positive": "Uses existing model hooks and bounded inference without fitting additional predictors.",
      "negative": "Correct target alignment, left-padding reconstruction, support accounting, and architecture-specific causality checks require careful implementation."
    }
  },
